This looks great. I've been impressed at how flexible CodeMirror is though. When I started EtherPK (
https://app.etherpk.com
) I made a bet that CodeMirror could help me build an editor that could combine outliner blocks, standard prose and rich overlays / graphics / embeds in one editor. I was concerned that I'd hit a road block (I presumed Logseq used custom HTML, nested divs and click position mapping for rendered text to the underlying input for a reason), but 9 months in I couldn't be happier with my choice, and it's a blazing fast editor that feels really nice and light to type in (live demo:
https://app.etherpk.com/demo
)

Here is a shameless plug for my project. It is a esp32 based programmable keyboard (HID) with a web interface and api. It can be directly flashed to the m5stack atom or m5stack cardputer.
One stand-out feature it has is an implementation of the Keepass KDBX database standard. The database can be stored on FRAM or SD card and can be unlocked via the web interface. The key is stored in volatile memory.
There is a DSL scripting language that can be used to run macros, programs, or functions. With the database unlocked you can send credentials via key-value DSL strings such as the following example:
{credstore  myusername}
{TAB}
{credstore mypassword}
{ENTER}
https://github.com/akoerner/kprox

